    "lawCode": "Amd §§6738, 6739, 6740 & 6742-a, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8746\n\nSPONSOR: STAVISKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to physical therapist\nassistants\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nUpdates the practice of physical therapist assistant from a credentialed\nto licensed profession.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSections 1 through 4 amend respectively sections 6738 (definition of\nphysical therapist assistant), 6739 (duties of licensed physical thera-\npist assistant and the use of the title \"physical therapist assistant\"),\n6740 (requirements as a physical therapist assistant) and 6742-a\n(continuing education) to update the physical therapist assistant\nprofession from certification to licensure.\n\nSection 5 sets forth an effective date of 18 months after enactment.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPhysical therapist assistants are professionals that work under the\nsupervision of physical therapists and are currently supervised and\nregulated by the State Education Department. Physical therapist assist-\nants are educated and trained to provide physical therapy care once a\ntreatment plan has been established by a physical therapist.  They are\ntrained clinicians who play an active role in the provision of physical\ntherapy care. Currently, physical therapist assistants are a certified\nprofession.\n\nPhysical therapist assistants, however, are equivalent to a licensed\nprofession rather than a certified one. Most significantly, physical\ntherapist assistants have a defined scope of practice performing phys-\nical therapy modalities under the supervision and direction of a phys-\nical therapist. Physical therapist assistants must meet statutorily\ndefined educational and examination requirements.\n\nOccupational therapist assistants, which play a similar role for the\nprofession of occupational therapy, are now a licensed profession.  The\nbill will correct the current statutory oversight and rightly define the\npractice of physical therapist assistant as a licensed profession.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019-20 - S. 4735/A. 3070 - Higher Education Committee.\n\n2018 - S. 8168 - Passed Senate/A. 10489 - Higher Ed Committee.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nEighteen months after enactment.\n",
